  frq: {
    prompt: 'Compare how a psychodynamic therapist, a person-centered therapist and a cognitive behavioral therapist would each approach a client with depression. Then explain how the continuum of care and the principle of the least restrictive environment guide where the client should be treated if her risk changes.',
    rubric: [['Describes psychodynamic approach (unconscious conflicts, early experiences, free association/insight)', ['unconscious', 'freud', 'insight', 'free association', 'childhood']], ['Describes person-centered approach (unconditional positive regard, empathy, genuineness, client-led)', ['rogers', 'unconditional positive regard', 'empathy', 'genuine']], ['Describes CBT approach (identify/challenge distorted thoughts, behavioral activation, homework, structured)', ['thought', 'distort', 'behavioral activation', 'homework', 'challenge']], ['Explains continuum and least restrictive environment with risk-based examples (outpatient, IOP, PHP, inpatient, crisis)', ['outpatient', 'intensive', 'partial', 'inpatient', 'least restrictive', 'risk']]],
    model: 'A psychodynamic therapist would explore how unconscious conflicts and early relationships shape her depression, using free association and the client-therapist relationship to build insight. A person-centered therapist, following Carl Rogers, would offer unconditional positive regard, empathy and genuineness, letting her lead and trusting her capacity for growth once she feels accepted. A cognitive behavioral therapist would use a structured, short-term approach: identifying automatic negative thoughts such as “I always fail,” challenging distortions, scheduling rewarding activities through behavioral activation and assigning homework. The continuum of care matches intensity to need: if she is safe and functioning, weekly outpatient therapy is appropriate; if symptoms worsen she might step up to an intensive outpatient or partial hospitalization program; if she develops a suicide plan with intent or cannot stay safe, crisis services and inpatient hospitalization are needed. The least restrictive environment principle means choosing the most independent setting that still keeps her safe, and stepping back down as she improves.',
  },
  sources: [['OpenStax Psychology 2e, 16.2 Types of Treatment', PSY + '16-2-types-of-treatment'], ['OpenStax Psychology 2e, 16.3 Treatment Modalities', PSY + '16-3-treatment-modalities'], ['SAMHSA: Recovery and Recovery Support', 'https://www.samhsa.gov/substance-use/recovery'], ['NAMI: Types of Mental Health Professionals', 'https://www.nami.org/about-mental-illness/treatments/types-of-mental-health-professionals/'], ['NIMH: Psychotherapies', 'https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/psychotherapies']],
